{"chunk_id":"R5-264419-003","collection":"ran5_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["China Unicom"],"tdocNumber":"R5-264419","title":"Discussion on AI-assisted Efficiency Enhancement for 6G Conformance Testing"},"point_id":"17947252121706818537"}
In this contribution we provided our initial thinking on the 5G-A test efficiency enhancement and 6G test and conformance requirement aspects and reached following proposals: Proposal 1: RAN5 can consider establishing an AI-assisted editorial and completeness checking capability for RAN5 pre-meeting CR review, as the beginning of the AI-assisted discussion. We call on the RAN5 group to jointly invest in this capability - companies with AI/ML expertise are encouraged to contribute tool development, and all contributors are welcome to provide test case examples and rules for training. RAN5 can benefit from AI-assisted pre-meeting checks that improve CR editorial quality and completeness. Proposal 2: RAN5 can consider establishing AI-assisted pattern inference for “pattern-driven” WI CR generation, as a pilot in the AI-assisted discussion. For work items that follow established patterns (e.g., HPUE power classes PC2/PC1.5), RAN5 can investigate whether AI tools can: - Learn the test case writing patterns from existing examples (e.g., the established PC2 test cases in TS 38.521-1). - For band combinations that have interested deployers (listed in PRD21) but no responsible company, auto-generate draft CRs following the learned patterns. - Introduce a mandatory human review mechanism where the generated CRs are reviewed and validated by domain experts before submission. The output quality should be evaluated against manually-written CRs to establish a baseline. Participation in such pilots is voluntary, and the scope is limited to combinations governed by the PRD21 guidelines. Proposal 3: RAN5 can consider establishing AI-assisted testing efficiency as a formal topic in RAN5's 6G readiness work, as part of the AI-assisted discussion. RAN5 can formally recognize AI-assisted workflow efficiency as part of its 6G preparation, parallel to the ongoing 6G specification modernization study (6GSM) [2][3]. Specifically: - Add "AI-assisted testing efficiency" as a standing discussion item in RAN5 meetings. - Collect contributions on AI tool capabilities, evaluation methodologies, and quality benchmarks. - Target: by the time 6G test specification work begins, have a mature set of AI-assisted tools and community-agreed processes in place.

